        <div id="chemistries" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Four Chemistries, Four Different Floors
          </h2>

          <p>
            Coatings in this category look similar in a photo and behave very differently on a slab. The label word that matters most is not the brand but the chemistry, because chemistry sets the cure speed, the film thickness, the UV stability and how much margin for error you get. If you only want a fresh color on a floor that sees feet, our guide to the <a href="/best-concrete-floor-paint/">best concrete floor paint</a> covers the simpler one-part route.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Water-based two-part epoxy</h3>

          <p>
            The entry point for real coatings, and what most big-box garage kits contain. Resin and hardener react to form a cross-linked film, but some of the volume is water that evaporates, so each coat ends up relatively thin. These kits are forgiving, low odor and inexpensive. They are tougher than any paint, but thinner and less durable than high-solids epoxy or polyaspartic.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">High-solids and 100% solids epoxy</h3>

          <p>
            Little or nothing evaporates, so the film you lay down is the film you keep. That allows thick builds in one or two coats and much better impact and abrasion resistance. These products are thicker to spread, often have short pot life in warm weather, and, being aromatic epoxies, tend to amber in sunlight unless topped with a UV-stable clear coat.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Polyaspartic</h3>

          <p>
            An aliphatic polyurea variant that cures quickly, resists UV yellowing and holds up very well to abrasion and chemicals. It is the standard for professional one-day garage floors. Working time can be very short, so DIY polyaspartic requires planning, small batches and a helper. It also costs more per gallon than epoxy.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Hybrids and penetrating sealers</h3>

          <p>
            Hybrids such as Rust-Oleum's polycuramine blend chemistries to get faster cure and better toughness than standard epoxy while staying DIY-friendly. Penetrating silane and siloxane sealers are a different category altogether: they form no film and protect the concrete from water and salt from within. Our guide to the <a href="/best-penetrating-concrete-sealer/">best penetrating concrete sealer</a> covers that family in depth.
          </p>

