          <p>
            One practical tip when comparing kits: look at the volume solids figure on the technical data sheet, not just the coverage claim on the box. Two kits that each claim to cover 500 square feet can leave very different dry film thicknesses behind. Higher solids at the same coverage means a thicker, tougher floor, and that number explains much of the price spread between budget and premium systems.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="choose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Matching the Coating to the Space
          </h2>

          <p>
            <strong>Daily-driver garage.</strong> Hot tires, road salt and dripping fluids are the hardest test a residential floor faces. A two-part epoxy kit is the minimum here, and a polyaspartic or hybrid system is better if the floor gets direct sun through the open door or you want to park on it within a day or two. Our full ranking of the <a href="/best-garage-floor-coating/">best garage floor coatings</a> covers garage-specific products, including flake systems and tile alternatives.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Workshop or hobby space.</strong> Dropped tools, rolling carts and solvent spills favor thick-build coatings. A 100% solids epoxy gives the impact resistance and chemical resistance these floors need, and the slower cure is rarely a problem in a shop you can close for a few days. Add a clear UV-stable topcoat if windows or doors let in direct sun.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Basements.</strong> Below-grade slabs breathe moisture vapor, which pushes film coatings off. Before committing to any two-part system in a basement, measure moisture. Many basements are better served by a breathable floor paint or a penetrating sealer; see our guide to the <a href="/best-basement-floor-paint/">best basement floor paint</a> for that side of the decision.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Outdoor slabs, patios and pool decks.</strong> Film coatings outside face UV, freeze-thaw and standing water. Epoxy is usually the wrong choice here because of UV and moisture issues. A one-part textured coating, or a penetrating sealer that leaves the concrete bare, is a better fit, and slip resistance should come first on any surface that gets wet.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE SHORTLIST</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Daily parking: polyaspartic or hybrid. Shop: thick epoxy. Outdoors: no epoxy.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">If you are unsure, the safest rule is that the more sun, moisture and heat the floor sees, the less suitable a standard epoxy becomes, and the more a UV-stable or breathable product earns its price.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="profile" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Grinding vs Etching: How Coatings Grip Concrete
          </h2>

          <p>
            Film coatings bond mechanically. They flow into the microscopic peaks and valleys of the concrete surface and lock in as they cure. A smooth, dense slab offers nothing to grab, which is why profile, not glue strength, determines whether a coating stays put. The industry describes surface texture using the ICRI concrete surface profile scale, which runs from CSP 1, nearly smooth, to CSP 10, very rough.
          </p>

          <p>
            Most thin and medium coatings want a CSP of roughly 2 to 3, about the feel of 80 to 120 grit sandpaper. Acid etching with citric or muriatic acid can get some slabs to around CSP 1 or 2, enough for many water-based epoxy kits on porous concrete. It cannot remove sealers, curing compounds or old coatings, and it is inconsistent on dense, power-troweled garage floors.
          </p>

          <p>
            Diamond grinding is how professionals profile floors, and it is what polyaspartic and high-solids epoxy manufacturers generally specify. A walk-behind grinder with a dust shroud and a matched HEPA vacuum removes the top skin of the slab, cuts through thin old coatings and sealers, and leaves a consistent profile. Rental shops commonly carry them, and grinding a two-car garage is typically a half-day job for a first-timer.
          </p>

          <p>
            Grinding produces fine concrete dust containing crystalline silica, which is a lung hazard. Use a grinder with a shroud connected to a vacuum, wear a properly fitted N95 or better respirator, and keep the door open for ventilation. Dry-sweeping ground concrete just puts the dust back in the air, so vacuum instead. These steps matter even for a single weekend project.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Profile at a Glance</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
