        <div id="moisture" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Moisture: The Rule That Overrides Every Label
          </h2>

          <p>
            Concrete is porous and slabs on grade constantly move water vapor upward from the soil. Film coatings resist vapor, so it builds up beneath them and pushes the film off as blisters, bubbles or large peeling sheets. Even the best coating will fail on a slab with too much moisture, and no amount of product quality compensates for that.
          </p>

          <p>
            The simple screening method is the plastic sheet test: tape a square of plastic to the floor for at least 16 hours, then look for condensation or darkened concrete underneath. It catches obvious problems. Coating manufacturers and installers use more precise methods, such as calcium chloride kits that measure moisture vapor emission rate or in-slab relative humidity probes, and many coatings list a maximum acceptable reading on their data sheet.
          </p>

          <p>
            If moisture is too high, there are three practical options. Solve the source: grading, gutters, drainage and sump systems for basements. Use a moisture-mitigating epoxy primer designed for the reading you have, which some systems offer as a base coat. Or choose a breathable product, such as a penetrating sealer, that does not trap vapor. Our guide to the <a href="/best-basement-wall-sealer/">best basement wall sealer</a> covers related below-grade waterproofing.
          </p>

          <p>
            New concrete adds another variable. Fresh slabs release a great deal of water as they cure, and most coatings require at least 28 days before application, often more for thick or impermeable systems. If your garage slab is new, plan the coating for next season rather than next month, and test moisture before you mix.
          </p>

          <p>
            Seasonal timing matters as well. Groundwater and slab moisture are usually highest in spring and after long rainy spells, and lowest in late summer and early fall. A slab that passes a moisture test in a dry August may read very differently in April. If you can, test during a wetter stretch, or test twice, so the coating is chosen for the worst conditions the floor will see rather than the best.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="install-day" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Install Day: Sequencing a Two-Part Floor
          </h2>

          <p>
            The day of application is about order and timing. Everything should be in place before you mix a single batch: the floor profiled, vacuumed and dry, edges taped, tools laid out, flakes measured into buckets, spiked shoes ready, and a plan for where you will start and exit. Once the resin and hardener meet, the pot life clock starts and pauses for nothing.
          </p>

          <p>
            Check the temperature and dew point. Most coatings want air and slab temperatures in a specified range, often from the 50s to the 80s Fahrenheit, with the slab at least 5 degrees above the dew point. Warm temperatures shorten pot life dramatically. Cooler conditions slow the cure. Avoid coating a slab that is in direct afternoon sun, since uneven heat can cause outgassing and bubbles.
          </p>

          <p>
            Mix in batches you can apply within the pot life, using a drill and mixing paddle at low speed for the time the instructions specify. Pour the mixed material onto the floor in a ribbon rather than leaving it in the bucket, where it heats up and cures faster. Cut in along walls first, then roll or squeegee the field in manageable sections, keeping a wet edge.
          </p>

          <p>
            If using flakes, broadcast them into the wet coat by tossing them up and letting them fall, rather than throwing them down, to get an even scatter. Full-broadcast floors get scraped and vacuumed after cure to remove loose flakes before the clear topcoat. Then follow the recoat and cure times. Foot traffic, light use and vehicle traffic each have separate wait times, which vary considerably by chemistry.
          </p>

