        <div id="failures" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Why Floor Coatings Fail, and What It Looks Like
          </h2>

          <p>
            <strong>Hot tire pickup.</strong> Tires heat up on the road, soften, and when parked bond to the coating. As the car pulls away, the coating lifts in tire-shaped patches. It happens most often with one-part paints and under-cured or thin coatings. Fully cured two-part systems over a properly profiled slab resist it far better.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Delamination.</strong> Large sections of coating that peel cleanly off the concrete almost always point to a bonding failure: a sealer or curing compound under the coating, a slab that was too smooth, residual oil, or dust left after grinding. The underside of the peeled coating often shows the cause, such as shiny residue or a layer of fine dust.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Blisters and bubbles.</strong> Small bubbles during application usually come from outgassing as the slab warms, rolling too fast, or mixing too aggressively. Blisters that appear weeks or months later, especially ones that weep water when popped, indicate moisture vapor from below. The first issue is cosmetic and sometimes fixable; the second means a moisture problem that needs addressing.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Yellowing and chalking.</strong> Aromatic epoxies amber and sometimes chalk when exposed to sunlight, which is most visible near open garage doors. It is cosmetic rather than structural. A UV-stable topcoat of polyaspartic or aliphatic urethane prevents it, which is why professional epoxy floors are so often finished with one.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. DIY vs Hiring a Pro: What Each Really Costs
          </h2>

          <p>
            At the time of writing, DIY water-based epoxy kits run roughly $100 to $250 for a one-car garage, with hybrid and higher-end kits somewhere around $300 to $700 for a two-car floor and polyaspartic or 100% solids systems often more. Add rental for a grinder and vacuum, roughly $100 to $250 per day, plus crack filler, respirator, spiked shoes and tools.
          </p>

          <p>
            Professional installations of flake polyaspartic or epoxy floors typically come in somewhere around $4 to $9 per square foot, which puts a two-car garage in the range of roughly $2,000 to $4,500 depending on region, slab condition and system. That price normally includes diamond grinding, crack repair, base coat, flakes and a clear topcoat, along with a warranty. Our breakdown of <a href="/epoxy-garage-floor-cost/">epoxy garage floor cost</a> goes into pricing in more detail.
          </p>

          <p>
            Professionals bring three things DIY rarely does: industrial grinders that create a consistent profile, experience with fast-curing materials that leave no room for error, and warranties that cover delamination. The real comparison is a DIY kit, a weekend and some risk versus a pro install, one or two days and far less risk. For polyaspartic in particular, a pro often makes sense.
          </p>

          <p>
            DIY makes most sense with a water-based epoxy or hybrid kit, a garage that is not your daily parking spot, and a slab that passes moisture and sealer tests. If any of those are not true, or if you want a full-broadcast flake floor with a polyaspartic topcoat, get at least two professional quotes before buying a kit.
          </p>

          <p>
            Hidden costs are worth adding up before you decide. A DIY floor usually means two or three days with the garage out of use, a grinder rental, about a dozen consumables, and the real chance of a redo if the slab surprises you. A professional crew usually finishes in one or two days and carries the risk. When the gap between your DIY budget and the lowest quote is small, the warranty alone can tip the decision.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="care" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Caring For a Coated Floor and Recoating Later
          </h2>

          <p>
            A good coating is low-maintenance but not no-maintenance. Sweep or dust-mop regularly, since grit carried in on tires and shoes slowly dulls the surface. Mop with a pH-neutral cleaner or a dilute ammonia solution as manufacturers often recommend, and avoid acidic or citrus-based cleaners and soap-heavy products that leave a slippery film.
          </p>

          <p>
            Clean spills promptly. Most two-part coatings resist oil, antifreeze and road salt, but brake fluid, battery acid and some solvents can etch or soften them if left to sit. In snowy climates, squeegee meltwater out of the garage instead of letting salty puddles sit, and put a containment mat under each car during winter.
          </p>

          <p>
            Protect the floor from point loads. Jack stands, kickstands, trailer tongues and heavy tool chests can dent or scratch even tough coatings. A square of plywood or a rubber pad spreads the load and saves the finish. Welding spatter and grinding sparks will burn through most coatings, so lay down a welding blanket for that kind of work.
          </p>

          <p>
            When the topcoat starts to dull or wear thin in traffic lanes, recoat before it wears through to the base. A light scuff-sand, thorough cleaning and a fresh clear topcoat restores gloss and protection at a fraction of the cost of redoing the floor. Coated floors that are maintained this way can last a decade or longer.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keep a small repair kit on the shelf. Most manufacturers sell their resin and topcoat in small quantities, and a chip from a dropped wrench can be cleaned, lightly sanded, filled with a dab of mixed coating and sprinkled with a few flakes. Repaired early, a ding stays a ding. Left open, water and salt get under the edge of the film and the damaged patch slowly grows outward each winter.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming a kit labeled epoxy is a two-part coating</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Many products marketed as epoxy floor paint are one-part acrylics with a small amount of epoxy resin. They are good paints but not true coatings, and they will not handle daily vehicle traffic the same way. Check the label for a separate hardener or Part B before you assume you are buying a cross-linked system.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Acid etching when the system calls for grinding</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Polyaspartic and high-solids epoxy systems are usually specified over a diamond-ground surface. Acid etch leaves an inconsistent profile on dense slabs and cannot remove sealers. Using etch where grinding is called for is one of the most common reasons a premium coating peels within the first year or two.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Mixing the whole kit at once</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Mixed coating keeps reacting in the bucket and generates heat that speeds the cure. Mixing more than you can spread within the pot life can leave you with a bucket of hardening material halfway across the floor. Mix in batches sized to what you can apply in the stated working time, and pour out promptly.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Coating over a slab with a moisture problem</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Moisture vapor will push off almost any film coating, sometimes within weeks. Spending hundreds of dollars on a premium system without testing first is the most expensive gamble in floor coating. Run at least a plastic sheet test and, for valuable projects, a calcium chloride or RH test before you buy.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the UV topcoat near open doors</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Standard epoxies yellow in sunlight, and the band of floor nearest an open garage door will often discolor first. A UV-stable clear topcoat of polyaspartic or aliphatic urethane prevents that and adds abrasion resistance. Skipping it saves a little money now and leaves an uneven, ambered floor later.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
