        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which concrete floor coating is the most durable?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                In residential garages, polyaspartic and high-solids epoxy systems topped with a polyaspartic or urethane clear coat are the most durable options. Polyaspartic has the edge in UV stability and fast cure, while thick epoxy offers excellent impact resistance. Durability depends heavily on prep, especially diamond grinding and moisture testing, so a properly installed epoxy can outlast a poorly installed polyaspartic.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Polyaspartic or epoxy: which is better?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Polyaspartic cures faster, resists UV yellowing and generally holds up better to abrasion and hot tires, which is why professional installers prefer it. Epoxy is cheaper, easier for DIYers to apply and builds thick films for impact resistance. Many professional floors use both: an epoxy base coat for build and bonding, and a polyaspartic topcoat for UV and wear resistance.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does concrete have to be ground before it is coated?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For polyaspartic and high-solids epoxy, manufacturers generally specify diamond grinding to create a consistent profile and remove contaminants. Some water-based epoxy kits allow acid etching on porous, unsealed slabs. If the floor has an existing sealer, old coating or is very smooth and dense, grinding is the reliable way to prepare it, regardless of product.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What lifespan should I expect from a floor coating?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A water-based DIY epoxy kit often lasts about three to six years in a residential garage, while professionally installed polyaspartic or thick epoxy floors with a clear topcoat commonly last ten years or more. The range depends on prep, moisture, traffic, sunlight and maintenance. Recoating the topcoat as it wears can extend life considerably.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I coat a basement floor with epoxy?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                You can, but only if the slab passes moisture testing. Basement slabs often move moisture vapor upward, which can cause epoxy to blister and peel. Test with a plastic sheet or calcium chloride kit first. If moisture is high, use a moisture-mitigating primer system, a breathable floor paint, or a penetrating sealer instead of a standard epoxy coating.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What causes hot tire pickup?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Hot tires soften and bond to the surface of a floor coating when parked, then lift it as they roll away. It is most common with one-part paints, thin coatings and coatings that have not fully cured. Thick two-part coatings over well-prepared concrete resist it, as does waiting the full vehicle cure time before parking on a new floor.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I coat my garage floor or just seal it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Coat it if you want a decorative, easy-to-clean surface that hides stains and brightens the space. Seal it with a penetrating sealer if you want protection from water, salt and freeze-thaw with no film to peel or maintain. In cold climates with heavy road salt, a penetrating sealer is a low-risk choice; for a showroom finish, choose a coating.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
